        -:   20:It is carried only where it can change the code the compiler emits: a loop
        -:   21:that stores through one pointer while loading through another, which is the
        -:   22:only thing the qualifier buys. perl is built with -fno-strict-aliasing, so
        -:   23:inside such a loop `restrict` is the *only* disambiguation available -- even
        -:   24:between an NV array and a size_t one, where the type rule would otherwise have
        -:   25:settled it -- and dropping it costs a reload of the other array every
        -:   26:iteration. Where there is nothing to disambiguate the annotation is left off:
        -:   27:a pointer whose every access is a perl API call (which clobbers memory
        -:   28:anyway), a read-only pointer in a function that stores through nothing, a
        -:   29:scalar out-parameter written once, an in-place sort with a single array, a
        -:   30:pointer that Renew() re-points, and a struct member, which neither gcc nor
        -:   31:clang exploits.
        -:   32:
        -:   33:Makefile.PL probes for a C99 flag but cannot always find one, so on some
        -:   34:compilers the keyword may not be recognised at all: MSVC accepts it only under
        -:   35:/std:c11 or later and otherwise spells the same guarantee __restrict, older gcc
        -:   36:spells it __restrict__, and a strict C89 compiler has no equivalent. Map it
        -:   37:where a spelling exists and define it away where none does. This has to sit
        -:   38:below every #include above, so that it cannot rewrite a parameter named
