        -:  110:/*Round an NV-valued intermediate back to the build's NV width.
        -:  111:
        -:  112:FLT_EVAL_METHOD is 2 on x87 -- 32-bit x86, and any build using -mfpmath=387 --
        -:  113:so an NV expression is evaluated in the 80-bit registers and rounded only when
        -:  114:it is spilled to memory. C99 says a cast or an assignment rounds, but gcc
        -:  115:honours that only under -fexcess-precision=standard, which -std=gnu99 does not
        -:  116:select; Makefile.PL now asks for it where the compiler takes the option, and
        -:  117:this is what the code relies on where it does not (an older clang, a vendor cc,
        -:  118:any compiler whose only C99 mode is a wide-register one). Assigning through a
        -:  119:volatile is the one portable way to force the store.
        -:  120:
        -:  121:Use it wherever a product is split into an integer part and a remainder, which
        -:  122:is where the extra bits change an answer rather than its last digit: 1000*0.007
        -:  123:is 7.000000000000000146 in an x87 register and exactly 7 once rounded to
        -:  124:double, so quantile() interpolated between two order statistics where R returns
        -:  125:one exactly, and ceil(0.1*100) came out 11 rather than 10 -- an off-by-one in
        -:  126:every bedroc()/auroc() bucket count. Reported by a CPAN smoker on
        -:  127:i686-linux-64int, perl 5.32.1, Stats-LikeR 0.31.
        -:  128:
        -:  129:Not a general-purpose rounding barrier: it costs a store and a load, so it does
        -:  130:not belong in an accumulation loop. The last-ulp drift that excess precision
        -:  131:causes there is what the build flag is for.*/
