        -:  152:/*Float classification for an NV: NaN, infinite, finite. Every obvious spelling
        -:  153:of these is wrong here.
        -:  154:
        -:  155:C99's isnan()/isinf()/isfinite() are type-generic macros, but where a platform
        -:  156:does not provide them they are plain double functions: narrowing a
        -:  157:large-but-finite long double into one of those reports it as infinite rather
        -:  158:than merely rounding it. That is the trap described above, with a worse failure
        -:  159:mode than losing mantissa bits.
        -:  160:
        -:  161:perl's Perl_isnan / Perl_isinf / Perl_isfinite were what this file used
        -:  162:through 0.298, and they are unusable for a different reason -- on any perl
        -:  163:before 5.22 the fpclass() route through perl.h has never compiled at all.
        -:  164:Configure defines HAS_FPCLASS on illumos/Solaris, and perl.h there writes
        -:  165:
        -:  166:    #define Perl_fp_class()      fpclass(x)                 <- no parameter
        -:  167:    #define Perl_fp_class_inf(x) (Perl_fp_class(x)==FP_CLASS_NINF||...)
        -:  168:
        -:  169:so any use of Perl_isinf is a "macro passed 1 arguments, but takes just 0"
        -:  170:error, and FP_CLASS_NINF/FP_CLASS_PINF are names no <ieeefp.h> defines: Solaris
        -:  171:spells those FP_NINF and FP_PINF. That is what broke 0.298 on OmniOS (CPAN
        -:  172:Testers, perl-5.20.2, gcc 9.3), and nothing here can catch it, because the
        -:  173:whole block is dead code on Linux and glibc, where Configure finds isinf().
        -:  174:perl 5.22 rewrote it; 5.10.1 and 5.12.5 still carry it verbatim, and this
        -:  175:module supports 5.10. Those same perls also define Perl_isinf as ((x)==NV_INF),
        -:  176:which misses -Inf, and Perl_isfinite as finite((NV)x), where the prototype
        -:  177:undoes the cast.
        -:  178:
        -:  179:So classify by comparison instead. NV_MAX is the largest finite NV -- DBL_MAX,
        -:  180:LDBL_MAX or FLT128_MAX, straight out of <float.h> -- so a non-NaN NV is
        -:  181:infinite exactly when it falls outside [-NV_MAX, NV_MAX]. These call no libm
        -:  182:and need no header perl.h has not already included, they are exact at every NV
        -:  183:width so there is nothing left to narrow, and taking the value through an NV
        -:  184:parameter is also what discards any x87 excess precision that a bare macro
        -:  185:would leave in play.
        -:  186:
        -:  187:What they do rely on is IEEE comparison semantics: a NaN compares false against
        -:  188:everything including itself. That holds by default on every compiler this
        -:  189:module targets, and is only lost under a -ffast-math-style flag, which no perl
        -:  190:is built with.*/
