        -: 1483:/*Decide a whole subtree without walking it, where that is possible.
        -: 1484:
        -: 1485:With rows 0..row-1 placed (their lgamma(t+1) terms already summed into
        -: 1486:cur_lc) and C_rem holding what is left of each column, every completion T
        -: 1487:of the table satisfies
        -: 1488:
        -: 1489:     log P(T) = const_term - cur_lc - S,
        -: 1490:     S = sum of lgamma(t_ij + 1) over the cells still to be filled,
        -: 1491:
        -: 1492:so bounding S bounds log P over the entire subtree.  lgamma(x+1) is convex,
        -: 1493:so Jensen puts a floor under S -- spreading a row (or column) total evenly
        -: 1494:is the cheapest it can ever be -- and a! b! <= (a+b)! puts a ceiling on it,
        -: 1495:since piling a total into one cell is the dearest.  Rows and columns each
        -: 1496:yield both bounds; the tighter of the two is used.
        -: 1497:
        -: 1498:If even the most probable completion is already at or below the observed
        -: 1499:table's probability then every completion counts, and their combined mass
        -: 1500:has a closed form.  Counting the N' = sum(C_rem) remaining observations two
        -: 1501:ways -- assigned directly to rows, or column by column -- gives
        -: 1502:
        -: 1503:     sum over completions of prod 1/t_ij!
        -: 1504:         = N'! / ( prod_{i>=row} R_i!  prod_j C_rem_j! )
        -: 1505:
        -: 1506:which is the whole subtree in a single exp() instead of a walk over it.  If
        -: 1507:even the least probable completion sits above the threshold, nothing in the
        -: 1508:subtree counts and it is dropped outright.  Both tests are exact: they skip
        -: 1509:only work the enumeration would have done, and never change the sum.
        -: 1510:
        -: 1511:Returns 1 if the subtree was added whole, 2 if it was discarded whole, and
