        -: 1882:/*Design-matrix construction, shared by lm() and glm().
        -: 1883:
        -: 1884: A model term is a set of variables: "wt" is one, "wt:hp" is two, and a
        -: 1885: variable holding strings is a factor that expands to indicator columns. The
        -: 1886: question each factor raises is whether to emit a column for every level or to
        -: 1887: drop the first as a reference, and R answers it with the margin rule:
        -: 1888:
        -: 1889:   The factor f inside term T is coded by contrasts -- reference level dropped
        -: 1890:   -- when T with f removed is itself a term of the model, and by full
        -: 1891:   indicators when it is not. The empty margin, which is what a main effect
        -: 1892:   reduces to once its own variable is removed, counts as present whenever the
        -: 1893:   model has an intercept. Without an intercept it counts as present only
        -: 1894:   after the first factor main effect has consumed it.
        -: 1895:
        -: 1896: That one rule reproduces R everywhere:
        -: 1897:
        -: 1898:   y ~ g          g's margin is empty and the intercept supplies it, so g is
        -: 1899:                  coded by contrasts: gb, gc.
        -: 1900:   y ~ g - 1      nothing supplies the empty margin, so g is coded in full:
        -: 1901:                  ga, gb, gc. This is the case that used to lose a level and
        -: 1902:                  fit a model forcing the reference group's fitted values to 0.
        -: 1903:   y ~ a + b - 1  a consumes the empty margin and is coded in full; b then
        -: 1904:                  finds it present and is coded by contrasts. Coding both in
        -: 1905:                  full would be rank deficient.
        -: 1906:   y ~ a * b      both main effects are present, so both components of a:b are
        -: 1907:                  coded by contrasts: aB:bY.
        -: 1908:   y ~ a:b        neither main effect is present, so both components are coded
        -: 1909:                  in full and the term spans the whole cross-classification.
        -: 1910:
        -: 1911:Terms are ordered by degree first, as R's terms() does, so that a margin is
        -: 1912:always decided against terms that precede it.*/
