        -: 2639:/*Pearson product-moment r between two n-element arrays.
        -: 2640:Returns NAN when either variable has zero variance (matches R).*/
        -: 2641:/*Pearson correlation, two-pass about the sample means.
        -: 2642:
        -: 2643:The one-pass form this replaces -- (n*sxy - sx*sy) / sqrt((n*sx2 - sx^2) *
        -: 2644:(n*sy2 - sy^2)) -- subtracts two nearly equal large numbers in each of its
        -: 2645:three terms, so it loses a digit of the answer for every digit by which the
        -: 2646:mean exceeds the spread.  On a column with mean 1e9 and spread 0.05 it does
        -: 2647:not merely lose precision: n*sx2 - sx*sx comes out negative, the sqrt is NaN,
        -: 2648:and cor() returns NaN for a perfectly ordinary pair of vectors.  R reports
        -: 2649:0.063454463733801467 for that case (t/var_sd_cov.R.t, offset_1e9).
        -: 2650:
        -: 2651:Centring first is what R does too -- cov.c sums (x - xm)*(y - ym) and the two
        -: 2652:centred squares, never the raw cross-products -- and the trailing correction
        -: 2653:terms are the same compensated two-pass identity nv_cov2() uses, with the
        -: 2654:common 1/(n-1) cancelling between numerator and denominator.
        -: 2655:
        -: 2656:No restrict: the matrix branch of cor() calls this once per column pair, and
