        -: 7532:/*Inverse regularized incomplete beta (R's qbeta): incbeta is monotone in x,
        -: 7533:so safeguarded bisection converges to full double precision.
        -: 7534:
        -: 7535:The stopping rule has to be relative, not absolute.  A Clopper-Pearson bound
        -: 7536:is routinely far below 1 -- x = 1 success in n = 1e9 trials puts the lower
        -: 7537:bound at 1e-12 -- and halting at an absolute width of 1e-15 leaves such a
        -: 7538:bound with only four or five correct digits.  So bisect until the bracket is
        -: 7539:narrow *relative* to where it sits, and stop early if the two ends become
        -: 7540:adjacent doubles (the guard also terminates the walk down to a denormal
        -: 7541:root, which no fixed iteration count would reach).  While lo is still 0 the
        -: 7542:bracket halves geometrically, so a root k orders of magnitude below 1 is
        -: 7543:reached in ~3.3k steps and then refined in ~53 more.*/
