        -: 9795:/*shared machinery for skew() and kurtosis()
        -: 9796:Both statistics are ratios of central moments, so both need the same one
        -: 9797:pass over the sample.  The recurrence is Welford's, carried up to the third
        -: 9798:and fourth moments (Terriberry).  What this buys over the textbook
        -: 9799:expansion in raw moments -- m3 = Sx^3/n - 3*xbar*Sx^2/n + 2*xbar^3 -- is
        -: 9800:everything: for a column of values around 1e7 (a lab value in the wrong
        -: 9801:units, a timestamp) Sx^3/n is ~1e21 while m3 is single digits, so that form
        -: 9802:cancels away every significant figure.  Centering first, whether in a
        -: 9803:second pass or by this recurrence, is what keeps the answer; the recurrence
        -: 9804:additionally needs no second look at the input, which matters because the
        -: 9805:input here may be a bare list on the argument stack.  m2..m4 hold the
        -: 9806:*sums* of the powered deviations, not the moments; callers divide by n.*/
        -: 9807:typedef struct {
        -: 9808:	NV mean, m2, m3, m4;
        -: 9809:	size_t n;
        -: 9810:} moment_acc;
