        -:10778:igamc() (chi-square tails), pf_upper() (oneway_test, anova), bt_qbeta() and
        -:10779:bt_pbinom_*() (binom_test) -- so exporting them adds surface, not risk.
        -:10780:
        -:10781:Two rules are followed throughout, and both are about the tail nobody watches:
        -:10782:
        -:10783:- A tail is never formed as 1 - the other tail. t is symmetric, so pt(q) is
        -:10784:  pt_upper(-q) and qt(p) is -qt_tail(p); incbeta() and igam()/igamc() each
        -:10785:  take the parameter order that makes the requested tail the one they compute
        -:10786:  directly. Subtracting from 1 costs every digit below NV_EPSILON, which is
        -:10787:  exactly the range a p-value is interesting in.
        -:10788:- p outside [0, 1], a non-positive df, and a NaN anywhere all return NaN
        -:10789:  rather than a plausible number, as R does.
        -:10790:
        -:10791:log.p is honest in one direction only, and the docs say so. For the q*
        -:10792:functions the argument is a log probability and exp() of it is exact enough to
        -:10793:reach quantiles the linear scale cannot name. For the p* functions the answer
        -:10794:is log() of the probability already computed, so a tail that underflowed to 0
        -:10795:logs to -Inf instead of reporting -800; R's own pt/pchisq/pf carry log_p
        -:10796:through the series and can. pnorm() is the exception and does carry it through
        -:10797:(c_pnorm's log_p), because R's Cody algorithm was ported whole.
        -:10798:
        -:10799:Accuracy is bounded by those underlying routines, which converge on a relative
        -:10800:1e-15 term cutoff regardless of NV width -- the same situation c_pnorm's
        -:10801:double-precision port is already documented as having. So these agree with R to
        -:10802:about 1e-14 relative on a double, long-double and __float128 build alike, and
        -:10803:the tolerances in t/distributions.R.scipy.t are set from the worst observed
        -:10804:disagreement rather than from the NV width.*/
