        -:16546:	/*Negative binomial: alternate an IRLS fit at the current theta with a fresh
        -:16547:	ML estimate of theta at the current fitted means, exactly as MASS::glm.nb
        -:16548:	does. Every other family runs the body once.
        -:16549:	
        -:16550:	Three details of glm.nb decide whether the answers agree, and all three
        -:16551:	are reproduced below:
        -:16552:	
        -:16553:	 - The FIRST pass is an ordinary Poisson fit, not a negative-binomial one
        -:16554:	   at some large stand-in theta. Its fitted means are what the first theta
        -:16555:	   is estimated from, and its residual degrees of freedom set d1.
        -:16556:	 - Each later pass is WARM STARTED from the previous pass's means
        -:16557:	   (glm.nb passes etastart = log(mu)), so the fit it lands on is the one
        -:16558:	   MASS lands on rather than merely the same optimum reached from
        -:16559:	   elsewhere.
        -:16560:	 - Inside the loop theta is re-estimated from the means that STARTED the
        -:16561:	   pass, not the ones the pass just produced: glm.nb calls
        -:16562:	   theta.ml(Y, mu) and only then reassigns mu <- fit$fitted.values. The
        -:16563:	   lag is easy to miss and moves theta in the eighth digit.
        -:16564:	
        -:16565:	The convergence test is MASS's as well:
        -:16566:	
        -:16567:	    (|Lm0 - Lm| / d1 + |theta - theta_prev| / d2) < epsilon
        -:16568:	
        -:16569:	with d1 = sqrt(2 * max(1, df.residual)) from the Poisson pass, d2 = 1 and
        -:16570:	epsilon = 1e-8. What used to be here was a relative test on the
        -:16571:	log-likelihood alone -- |dll| < 1e-7 * (|ll| + 0.1) -- which on an
