        -:19326:"bedroc - Boltzmann-Enhanced Discrimination of ROC (Truchon & Bayly 2007)\n"
        -:19327:"\n"
        -:19328:"Early-recognition metric: scores actives ranked near the TOP of the list\n"
        -:19329:"far more than actives buried deep.  Result is in [0, 1] (1 = ideal early\n"
        -:19330:"recognition, 0 = worst, ~0.5 = random-ish depending on alpha & R_a).\n"
        -:19331:"\n"
        -:19332:"USAGE\n"
        -:19333:"  my $r = bedroc(\\@scores, \\@labels, alpha => 20);\n"
        -:19334:"  print $r->{bedroc};\n"
        -:19335:"\n"
        -:19336:"ARGUMENTS\n"
        -:19337:"  \\@scores      ranking scores (higher = better by default)\n"
        -:19338:"  \\@labels      class labels, OR a numeric column when cutoff/active_frac\n"
        -:19339:"                is given (then bedroc binarizes it for you)\n"
        -:19340:"  alpha => 20   early-recognition weight, > 0 (Truchon-Bayly default 20)\n"
        -:19341:"  positive => 1 label value that marks an active (string compare)\n"
        -:19342:"  cutoff => x   define actives as \\@labels entries with value >= x\n"
        -:19343:"  active_frac=>f binarize \\@labels: take a fraction f in (0,1) as active,\n"
        -:19344:"                from one tail (see active_side).  Exactly ceil(f*N) actives,\n"
        -:19345:"                clamped so both classes exist -- never dies for lack of a\n"
        -:19346:"                pre-built 0/1 label.  Mutually exclusive with cutoff.\n"
        -:19347:"  active_side=>  with active_frac: 'high' (default) = largest values are\n"
        -:19348:"    'high'      active (like cutoff); 'low' = smallest values (e.g. actives\n"
        -:19349:"                = strongest binders when the column is dG).\n"
        -:19350:"  direction=>'>' '>' higher score ranks first (default); '<' flips\n"
        -:19351:"  top => 0.05   also report enrichment in the top fraction (0..1]\n"
        -:19352:"\n"
        -:19353:"Unlike the common Python implementations (which need a pre-built 0/1 label\n"
        -:19354:"array, or reimplement a regression variant per script), active_frac lets one\n"
        -:19355:"call turn a raw measured column straight into a BEDROC score.\n"
        -:19356:"\n"
        -:19357:"RETURNS a hashref: bedroc, alpha, rie, rie_min, rie_max, n, n_active,\n"
